<<^ code=`&#x0002;`>>

```toml meta
cacheable = true
hydrate   = true
l-space   = "stable"
role      = "the container boundary as law — one image that carries code and nothing of a household; two mounts, /workspace for bags and wikis and /state for vessel homes and custody; the local socket kept inside the container with the host CLI entering through `docker compose exec`; engine control kept on the host and no docker socket for the app; LAN discovery that offers unauthoritative candidates while a pinned admission alone grants authority; a TLS terminator in front of a vessel that names its reach face from one setting; harnesses that run the image by digest; a release manifest that binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs; no pave or wipe command"
title     = "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor"
type      = "text/memetic-wikitext+tiddlywiki"
uri-path  = "ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor"
```

<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Harbor ~ the container boundary

A Lararium runs inside a container. This carrier states where the container ends and the household begins: what the image carries, what arrives as a mount, which side holds the socket, which side controls the engine, how a vessel gets found on a LAN, how a browser reaches it over HTTPS, and what a release names.

> ''Register.'' The operator ruled that all code runs dockerized, that one Lararium serves one human, that local-first comes first, and that no pave or wipe command exists (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). The house settled the boundary below under those rulings. The road lists what the build still owes (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road`>>, HARBOR).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/code-and-hooks`>>

!! Code in the container, hooks on the host

''All code runs dockerized.'' The daemon, the CLI, the web surface and every harness run from the image. The host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment:

* ''files'': the two mounts of #/the-two-mounts;
* ''session capture'': read-only binds of the session roots the operator names, one directory each, never a home directory whole;
* ''the editor and the CLI entry'': a host shim that calls `docker compose`, and the editor that opens files under `/workspace`;
* ''custody'': the state directory and its backup.

A host shim holds no logic of the vessel. It routes a verb to the container and refuses by name when a mount stands undeclared.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-image`>>

!! The image ~ code and genesis, nothing of a household

''One multi-arch image serves every surface'': a parent's desktop, a Linux box, a Pi, a VPS herm. Differences between surfaces ride the environment and the mounts, never a second image and never a second compose file per surface.

* The image carries the daemon, the CLI, the built web surface and the genesis seed with its content-addressed blobs.
* The image carries no corpus, no vessel home, no key and no credential. No layer, build argument or environment variable holds any of them.
* The image carries no test door. A harness that founds without a terminal mounts the staging tools read-only from the repository, and those tools act only on a root the harness marked.
* The vessel runs as a non-root user whose uid and gid the environment supplies, so files in both mounts stay editable by the host operator.
* The image declares both mount points as volumes, so a run that forgets a mount lands in an anonymous volume and never in a layer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-two-mounts`>>

!! The two mounts ~ `/workspace` and `/state`

| Mount | Holds | Law |
|---|---|---|
| `/workspace` | `bags/` and `wikis/`, the corpus the disk projection reads and writes | editable from the host; the projection's mirror roots resolve under it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection`>>) |
| `/state` | the vessel's homes and its custody | one directory per vessel; backed up apart from the corpus; never an image layer |

* ''One Lararium per human, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face; a session wears a face and the vessel wears none.
* ''One holder per store.'' The store takes its own claim on the state mount before it opens. A second holder on the same `/state` refuses `StoreHeld`, whichever container, namespace or entrypoint it came through.
* ''Explicit roots.'' `LAR_ROOT` names `/state`, and the daemon takes `--root /workspace`. `LAR_BAGS` alone moves the bags root only and leaves the mirror roots where they stood.
* ''Both mounts rest on a local filesystem the host trusts to honor `fsync`.'' A network share or a 9P bind changes ownership semantics and may refuse the hard link the socket claim makes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-socket`>>

!! The socket stays inside

The daemon and the CLI meet at a Unix socket in the container's own `/tmp`, a tmpfs. That socket never binds to the host, never crosses between two containers and never rests on a shared filesystem.

* ''The host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`.'' The exec'd CLI shares the daemon's `/tmp`, uid and environment, so it reaches the one socket the daemon holds.
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over that socket; when no vessel stands, the command owns the store itself for that one act, in a one-shot container on the same two mounts. A second process never opens the store beside a live holder.
* ''A credential rides a terminal alone.'' A founding and every custody verb gather their human route on an attached TTY. No flag and no environment variable carries one; without a TTY the CLI refuses `CredentialTtyRefusal`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-engine`>>

!! Engine control stays with the host

''The app never receives the docker socket.'' No container of this house mounts it, and no shim passes it. A process holding that socket holds the host.

So every act on the engine belongs to the host shim: standing the vessel, stopping it, restarting it, reading its log. When a vessel's standing no longer matches its state on disk (a face lit after boot), the CLI inside the container reports that verdict and the host shim performs the restart. The image declares that the host holds the engine (`LAR_ENGINE=host`), and under that setting every CLI door that would stop, signal or spawn a daemon refuses `EngineHeldByHost`. An acting `vessel stand` that meets that refusal answers `ok: false` and exit 3, since the act asked for did not happen, and still carries the standing verdict the shim reads (`data.standing.act`).

''A vessel stands holding no key, and the shim says so.'' Every start returns a vessel to the floor. After each stand and each restart it performs, the shim runs `vault unlock` attached when its shell holds a terminal, and otherwise names that one command; a refused or abandoned unlock leaves the vessel standing at the floor and never fails the act that stood it. `vault lock` passes through: the standing vessel drops its key and stands at the floor again. The shim reads no credential and carries none.

The container drops every Linux capability and forbids new privileges. It publishes its port on loopback unless the operator names a wider interface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/lan-discovery`>>

!! LAN discovery ~ candidates, never authority

Two vessels on one household LAN may find each other without typing an address. Discovery answers one question alone: //which addresses might host a vessel//.

* ''An announcement carries a bare service and a port.'' It carries no catalog address, no Nexus name, no key and no label that names a person or a house. Anyone on the LAN reads an announcement, so it holds nothing worth reading.
* ''A candidate grants nothing.'' The listener gains a URL to try. The gate at that URL answers only a dialer who already holds its pinned key (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/decisions`>>, the knock), so a candidate dialed with a wrong key meets silence.
* ''A pinned admission alone grants authority.'' The key reaches the joining device through an admission a human carried: a QR code or a `lar:` URI holding the URL and the gate key. A right key still wants the host's explicit admit before any plane opens.
* ''The fallback needs no multicast.'' The same QR code or URI carries the URL itself, so a LAN that drops multicast (guest isolation, a mesh of access points, a routed segment) loses convenience and nothing else.
* ''No clock decides.'' A candidate that stops answering simply fails the next dial; no timer marks a peer lapsed.

A container on the engine's default bridge network sends and hears no LAN multicast. Announcing from inside a container requires host networking or a macvlan attachment; each widens what the container reaches. The compose file therefore keeps the bridge by default, and the host-network choice stays an explicit, documented setting the operator turns.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/household-https`>>

!! Household HTTPS ~ a terminator in front, one declared name

A browser mints keys only in a secure context, so a phone on the LAN needs an `https` origin its trust store already accepts (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`>>). The boundary places that concern outside the vessel:

* ''A TLS terminator stands in front of the vessel'' and holds the wildcard certificate. The vessel serves plaintext to the terminator alone and keeps its loopback publication.
* ''One split-horizon resolver'' on the always-on node answers each household name with its own LAN address. The operator keeps one records file, one `<LAN address> <name>` line per name, so two hearths answer with two addresses. A name under the zone that the file does not list answers "no such name", so no household name leaves the house; a reverse lookup of a private, loopback or link-local address answers "no such name" inside the house as well; every other name forwards upstream. A one-shot check reads the file as the vessel's own uid before the resolver stands, so a file only that uid reads serves; it refuses by name a line the resolver would answer wrongly or never (an address octet led by a zero among them, since the resolver loads no zone that holds one), and renders the zone the resolver serves; a refused edit leaves the resolver on the last zone the check passed. No device installs anything.
* ''The vessel names its reach face from one setting'', `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. An `https` name carries `wss` for the relay, and the crossing URL the vessel prints (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos`>>) uses that same name throughout.
* ''The operator supplies the domain and the DNS credential; a one-shot issuer reads it.'' The credential rests as two owner-read files in a host directory the operator names, outside both mounts of #/the-two-mounts. The issuer service alone mounts that directory, read-only, asks for one wildcard certificate per machine and exits. It asks public resolvers for the zone's apex and its challenge record, never the household resolver, and refuses by name a resolver it reads as inside the house (a private, loopback, link-local or shared address, the resolver's own service name, a local name or a name under the zone): the household resolver answers every name under the zone itself, so through it the issuer would find the house's own apex and no name server. A record the zone publishes under the household name stays out of reach inside the house. No credential value enters the image, the compose file, an environment variable, `/state` or `/workspace`, and the vessel's container mounts none of it.
* ''The first always-on node issues; a second machine receives its certificate by hand.'' One node holds the credential. Every other machine gets a certificate and key a human carried to it.
* ''Issuance and renewal sit at the edge of the causal island.'' A certificate's validity window belongs to the browser's trust decision. It decides no admission, no revocation and no act inside the vessel.

The terminator, the resolver and the issuer attach through profiles in the compose file that stay off until the operator turns them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-harnesses`>>

!! The harnesses stand on the image

A harness proves the code a household runs only when it runs that code. So the mesh, kahu, crossing, swarm and burn harnesses run the built image, named by digest, and bind no source tree into a container. A harness may mount scratch state, scratch workspace and the staging tools; it mounts nothing that replaces the image's code. A browser runner needs a browser engine the product image lacks, so it runs from a browser image. The mesh's browser vessels still read their code from the product image, through a volume that image fills. One exception stands declared: the browser CI runner reads the repository read-only. A harness run names its own compose project, so two runs on one host share no container, network or volume.

Every boot rests at the floor holding no vessel key, and a harness holds no terminal. A harness opens a vessel it staged through the test custody door alone (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`; `tools/unlock-staged-container.sh` carries that door into a standing container), and the door refuses any root that carries no scratch marker. A harness reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key. A harness that founds a vessel of its own, such as the reader the Herm mesh witness dials with, stages that root and founds its custody through the same door before the founding runs.

The container drivers ride hosted runners on every pushed head (`.github/workflows/held-out.yml`). One job builds the product image and hands it on beside its digest; every cell that runs containers loads that image and refuses any other digest. Each driver that walks independent scenarios unfolds into one cell per scenario, and the scenario script's own list names them, so no cell stands written by hand. Each cell names its own compose project and records its own verdict; a merge job holds every verdict against the cell list and reads a red cell, a silent cell and a verdict for another head as red, each by name. `tools/ci-verdict.mjs` prints that run as its third reading, so a head no container driver measured reads unread.

''The island-door inventory measures reach, never policy.'' `tools/capability-inventory-witness.sh` reads, on the built tree, which of the four vessel doors (fetch, rest, custody write, dyad) each island class reaches, against the committed table `tools/capability-inventory.json`. A cell answers one question: does an ask from that class arrive at a door at all. On node every door stands as a stand-in that grants every ask and counts each call; what the real door then answers a real ask, a miss, a refusal or `aside`, stays outside the table. Each row drives the real code that builds its class's handle: a pool's own host spawn for the wiki and session islands, on node, and over a stand-in Web Worker in the browser, where the row reads every reply and counts the listeners the spawn attached. A row the tool cannot drive whole outside a browser reads `unmeasured` in every cell, never `silent`; the browser's daemon island reads so, since its spawn seam stands inside the vessel's open. A session island's view asks run through the pool's own listener, which no row drives: on node the content its view passes ends at the fetch door, behind that view's gate. Two source readings hold what no row drives, each counted per occurrence within a file: every reference to a function that spawns, wraps or answers an island, with every raw worker constructor and every renamed import of one, and every quoted ask or answer type of the doors and the view.

''A house's own island is proven, never inferred'' (road `P26`; controller ruling 2026-10-31). A vessel attaches each island's sync adapter itself at the island's spawn (a wiki or daemon island's port, `attachMessageChannelSync`; a session island's gated leg, `SessionDocumentView.attach`), and keeps, per Repo, a POSITIVE set of the peers that arrive on those adapters (`islandPeersOf`, mesh `island-repo.ts`), each proven by its ARRIVAL there: the island adapter marks the arrival before the network subsystem hears it (`markIslandArrival`), and a peer leaves the set at its disconnect, its port's close, or the arrival of its id on any other adapter (the subsystem takes a second arrival of a live id as a replacement; an island's own re-arrival on its adapter seats it again). A peer counts as the house's own only by membership in that set. Every other peer, on any adapter and in either direction, a hearth this vessel dialed included, takes the wire path, and the protected-document guard (`protectedDocGuard`, mesh `protected-doc-guard.ts`) reads first: a missing listening socket never proves a peer is in-process.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-release-manifest`>>

!! The release manifest

A release names one multi-arch image index and one record beside it. The record binds four content addresses together: the image digest, the genesis seed's CID, the engine CID and the grammar CID. A vessel, a harness or a second operator who holds the record can check that an image carries exactly the genesis, engine and grammar the release names, and refuse a mismatch before any vessel stands.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/no-pave`>>

!! No pave, no wipe

No verb of the host shim and no service of the compose file removes a state directory, a workspace or a volume that holds a vessel. The shim refuses, by name, every CLI verb that would clear a vessel's state. A fresh start happens by hand and stays reversible: the operator moves the old homes aside and founds again (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/fresh-start`>>). Stopping the vessel leaves both mounts as they stood.

''Local-first comes first.'' Nothing in the boundary forecloses a keyless ciphertext carrier on a cloud surface later: custody stays in `/state`, and a carrier that holds sealed bytes alone needs neither mount's contents in the clear.

<<~/ahu>>

